// What the pool does not yet do right, because the compiler does not, pinned
// as it is today. Every check here pins what happens TODAY, which is wrong,
// and says so in its label: when the compiler closes a gap, the check fails,
// and the right value is the one in the comment above it.
//
// THE HOLES, which compile today and dangle, so no check here can pin what
// they print. Written out so a reader knows what is not yet safe:
//
//   fn escapes() Option[&Pt] {
//     mut p = Pool[Pt].empty();
//     imm r = p.add(Pt[1, 2]);
//     p.get(r);                  // the pool dies here; the borrow in the
//   }                            // Option is returned and dangles
//
//   fn frozen() i64 {
//     mut p = Pool[Pt].empty();
//     imm r = p.add(Pt[1, 2]);
//     imm x = &mut p[r];
//     p.remove(r);               // nothing freezes the pool while x lives:
//     x.x;                       // x reads a finalized value
//   }
//
// The first: a borrow held inside another value carries no lifetime, so the
// borrow in 'get's and 'getMut's Option is not checked against the pool it
// came from. Returning the Option, storing it in an outer variable, and
// matching the borrow out of it and returning or storing that all compile.
// 'pool[r]' and '&mut pool[r]' return a bare borrow, which IS checked: the
// compiler's suite pins that returning one or storing it where it outlives
// the pool is refused (test/cases/region/region_flow_pool_escape.cone). So
// 'if pool.alive(r) { ...pool[r]... }' is the checked form today.
// The second is the missing freezing rule: 'remove', and an 'add' that grows
// the block (so moves every value), and moving or dropping the pool itself,
// all compile while a borrow from 'pool[r]', 'get' or 'getMut' is live.
// A static pool (a module's global) is never frozen by anything, even then.

mod gaps;

import testing use *;
import pool use *;

mut log = 0i64;
fn note(d i64) { log = log * 10i64 + d; }

struct Fin {
  pub n i64;
  fn final(self &uni) { note(n); }
}

// Right: 12, the value assigned over finalized when it is replaced
fn overwrite() {
  mut p = Pool[Fin].empty();
  imm a = p.add(Fin[1]);
  *(&mut p[a]) = Fin[2];
  expectInt(log, 0, "compiler gap: assigning through a reference does not finalize what it replaces");
}

fn main() i32 {
  overwrite();
  expectInt(log, 2, "compiler gap: the value assigned over is never finalized, only its replacement");
  done();
}
